WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.120
It's great to be joined today by Matt Gerth,
senior fellow at Media Matters, where his

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.000
work focuses on the Fox Trump feedback loop
news coverage of politics as well as media

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.000
ethics.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.009
Matt, really great to have you on.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:14.009
I appreciate it.

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:15.009
Good to be here.

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:20.269
So, I mean, let's just start with something
and maybe that can be the Dominion lawsuit

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:22.260
against Fox News.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.250
We have, of course, been covering with great
interest for me, one of the most interesting

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:32.149
storylines has been yet another layer to what
goes out on the airwaves at Fox.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:37.750
Doesn't necessarily match what the feelings
are among some of the hosts behind the scenes,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.500
and that's totally fine.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.660
But what's the bigger story, if there is one
yet about how the audience of Fox News is

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.200
reacting to this lawsuit and the leak texts?

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.590
If they are even aware of it, because this
is something I've been talking to my audience

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.590
about.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.980
Well, will the average FOX, newer Fox News
viewer even know what it is that is going

00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:59.000
on?

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.160
I think you've hit the nail on the head there.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.780
That's really the question.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:10.720
I mean, as we know, people who follow Fox
News are really in a bubble of right wing

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:11.720
media.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.380
They are getting their news solely from outlets
like Fox.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:23.549
They are disinclined to believe what they
hear from people like you and me and The New

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.360
York Times and The Washington Post and everybody
else.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:36.390
And that's something that Fox has really cultivated
for decades, trying to make its viewers not

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.220
believe other sources.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:45.710
I think what we are seeing, though, is some
right wing outlets are trying to get the news

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:50.400
out in hopes of building up their own audiences.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:58.829
That internal rivalry within the right could
be causing Fox some trouble as outlets like

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:08.420
Newsmax try to hive off a little bit of Fox's
market share by revealing what Fox hosts and

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.770
executives were saying behind the scenes.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:17.590
We know that Donald Trump has been arguing
that now Fox News isn't doing as well in the

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:22.930
ratings, he would argue, because they are
no longer as positive about him as they once

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:23.930
were.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.250
And we'll get to the narrative about Trump
on Fox more recently.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:31.810
But as far as the ratings go, is there any
truth to that claim that Fox is not doing

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.099
as well?

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:37.319
I don't think specifically.

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:38.739
It's easy to make that claim right now.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:45.810
I mean, in general, there's been a drop off
in cable news viewership since the last election,

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.810
right?

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:52.620
I would expect that to pop up a little bit
more as we get closer to the next presidential

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.150
election.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:02.819
But right now, I don't think we're seeing
a evaporation of Fox's market share due to

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:03.819
it.

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:08.530
Not supporting Trump enough, though, as we
saw in the Dominion filings, they are very,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:17.790
very attuned to that possibility and are willing
to take fairly substantial action in order

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.980
to prevent that from coming to pass.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:26.230
So there's a survey which suggests that about
a fifth of Fox News viewers now say they trust

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.269
the network less since the revelations from
the text messages.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:32.010
This is this is pretty squishy.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.370
It doesn't strike me as the most scientific
claim.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.659
And there's some definitional questions here.

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:39.990
And how much did they trust them before?

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.080
They probably still trust Fox way more than
you or I would.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.310
So there's sort of some squishiness here.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:51.480
But if we can believe that there is a portion
of the Fox audience that is now less and less

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.530
trusting of Fox News, what specifically would
it be?

00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:00.030
Would it be that they feel the hosts are telling
me one thing but privately believe something

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.030
else?

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.590
Or like what would be the practical belief
that's affecting them?

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.579
Yeah, that's the real question.

00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:12.290
And something that the poll can't really get
to is, is why they trust Fox less.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:25.389
Is it because they think Fox was lying to
them on TV or that they think Fox was not

00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:29.680
sufficiently supportive privately?

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:38.070
Is it because Fox is too into election denial
or not intuitive enough is something we can't

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.770
really pass out of that poll, though.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:48.870
I mean, I do think to a large extent what
Fox fears the most is other rivals on the

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:49.870
right.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:58.540
And so it is bad for them if Newsmax or Oann
can make the case that they are the real pro-Trump

00:04:58.540 --> 00:04:59.789
pro.

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:05.740
Denial outlet and Fox's audience turns to
them instead.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.580
What is the landscape, by the way, right now
of what we would consider the biggest competitors

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:15.460
to to Fox in the context in which I asked
that is one in which many of us in this business

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.040
we know about Newsmax.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.540
Newsmax has chosen to stream live 24 seven,
I believe, on YouTube.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.639
They're also on a number of cable systems.

00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:30.100
Oann famously has had issues where I think
they've been they've been non renewed by DirecTV

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:31.100
and maybe one other.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:36.130
I don't think you Oann is currently on YouTube
and that may be because they were kicked off,

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.950
although the details sort of escape me at
this point.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.789
What what does the competitive landscape look
like right now?

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:44.480
Yeah, that's definitely an issue.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:53.480
I mean, I think, you know, the TV landscape
is, I think, less robust, if anything, than

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.580
it was during the 2020 election cycle.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:03.190
You had both Newsmax and Oann as, as you mentioned,
losing access to some cable systems being

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:10.780
generally less available to the public at
the same time, as the TV audience deteriorates,

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.660
some of the digital and streaming options
have gotten stronger.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:23.240
So you see outlets like The Daily Wire or
The Blaze are picking up some steam and those

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:27.210
are competing for the same audience.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.830
If not showing up in the Nielsen ratings or
what have you.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.990
And what's the deal with Real America's Voice?

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.940
I see clips very often, but it's not clear
to me whether it does that even have a cable

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.220
channel that is available.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.910
Aside from whether anyone's carrying it or
is it online only?

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.389
It's mainly online.

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:49.410
I think there's some cable availability, but
not a ton.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:59.139
That said, no, as cord cutting continues to
be a real business imperative for these networks.

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:02.229
Streaming does become somewhat more viable
and important.

00:07:02.229 --> 00:07:06.539
So let's shift a little now and talk about
the primary coverage that we've seen of the

00:07:06.539 --> 00:07:08.430
Republican primary.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.930
My assessment right now, and you'll be able
to give us some kind of more fine tuned assessment

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:20.120
was that before the news of the looming arrest
of Donald Trump, it seemed as though Fox News

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:24.729
was sort of creating a little bit of space
for themselves in the direction of Ron DeSantis.

00:07:24.729 --> 00:07:30.949
There were some, you know, men on the street,
aka Brian KILMEADE, in a diner, segments that

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:34.740
seemed tailored to find people that that wanted
Ron DeSantis to run.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:38.600
They did Some actual man on the street segments
were like seven of the eight people they included

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.750
in the segment said that they want to Santos
there.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.670
Steve Doocy has been sporadically a little
bit more critical of Donald Trump, and it

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:48.210
sort of seemed like it was a soft escape hatch.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:52.321
So if it's the Santos, they have a credible
path to have access, but if it's Trump, they

00:07:52.321 --> 00:07:56.210
can kind of revert back and maintain the access
that they want.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.020
We were talking before the interview.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:02.620
You said that that may have shifted back a
little bit since the news of the looming arrest

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:03.639
of Trump.

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:09.610
Yeah, I think what we tend to see with Fox
and Trump is they often have these sorts of

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.940
squabbles where the network will try to create
a little bit of distance.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:18.490
Trump will lash out at the network for not
being supportive enough, but in the end, they

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:25.570
always come back to one another because their
relationship is so valuable to both of them.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:31.900
Because Fox is a very effective propaganda
tool for Trump, and Trump has a real hammerlock

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.229
on the Fox viewership.

00:08:35.229 --> 00:08:43.419
And so when we had we had a similar sort of
sense that Fox and the broader Murdoch universe

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:46.390
were trying to break away last year.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:54.000
But then the FBI raid on Mar a Lago really
sent the Fox hosts back into Trump's corner

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:00.950
and news that an indictment may be pending
for Donald Trump has created a similar situation.

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:08.240
You have FOX hosts like Sean HANNITY and Tucker
Carlson really rallying around the former

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.620
president, saying that any indictment of him
is an attack on his supporters, which obviously

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:22.500
means their viewers, that it would be un-American
and a sign that we're in a banana republic.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:29.829
So, you know, they're certainly not planning
on using any indictment as a way to bolster

00:09:29.829 --> 00:09:32.710
some other candidate in his stead.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:39.100
It really seems like they've they've fully
committed back to his side right now.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:44.130
How should we expect the primary to go, assuming
Trump stays in it and that the arrests slash

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.760
indictments don't don't lead to him dropping
out?

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:52.210
If there is a real contested primary here,
should we expect some version of what we've

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:58.149
been seeing throughout until it becomes clear
who is likely to be the nominee or since Donald

00:09:58.149 --> 00:09:59.149
Trump continues to?

00:09:59.149 --> 00:10:04.190
A poll so strongly at this point in time,
is it likely that there will be some kind

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.280
of of shift?

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.089
I'm trying to think of what what we should
expect as far as that goes.

00:10:09.089 --> 00:10:14.720
Yeah, I mean, Trump's polling has strengthened
considerably since late last year when it

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.680
really looked like DeSantis was making a run.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:25.410
And you could see the possibility that there
would be a sort of coalescing around him.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:33.130
But if that doesn't really happen, if DeSantis
fades and it's Trump with 50 to 60% in the

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:40.630
polls against a bunch of people with, you
know, 10% or less, it's really hard to imagine

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:48.450
the various tentacles of the right wing not
coming back under his his wing.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:57.839
I mean, he's going to have so much of the
energy at that point that it will become sort

00:10:57.839 --> 00:11:05.160
of business imperative for them like it was
after he became the nominee in 2016 to find

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.690
a way to be on his side.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:12.839
What do you make of the potential impact of
the Dominion suit?

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:17.589
Because from a business standpoint, there
are some pieces that argue, yeah, it's quite

00:11:17.589 --> 00:11:21.709
possible that Rupert Murdoch's going to have
to cut a pretty large check, but he can do

00:11:21.709 --> 00:11:25.360
that and it won't really have an impact on
the network.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.210
Maybe there will be part of an agreement.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:32.459
Could be this is all hypothetical that some
number of corrections need to be issued on

00:11:32.459 --> 00:11:34.070
air or something like that.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:35.500
We've seen that in other situations.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.850
Situations, but maybe not.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:42.680
Do you think that the actual outcome, aside
from the fact that the texts were released

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:49.230
and now we know what's in them, will impact
Fox News's viewership one way or the other?

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:50.649
I don't know about its viewership.

00:11:50.649 --> 00:11:54.970
I do think that on the business side, things
could get very dicey if Rupert Murdoch has

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.780
to cut a very large check over this.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:07.149
There's going to be, I think, a substantial
push from other shareholders to sue the network

00:12:07.149 --> 00:12:10.560
for various malfeasance cases.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:20.050
I think that's going to pose a real problem
for Murdoch going forward, assuming that he

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.389
does have to write that check.

00:12:21.389 --> 00:12:26.959
And, you know, that's always it's sort of
tricky to make predictions around defamation

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:33.209
lawsuits, though Obviously, this does seem
pretty strong as they go.

00:12:33.209 --> 00:12:38.220
When it comes to just more generally coverage
of the Biden administration.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:44.670
It seems almost like there are enclaves on
Fox News where you see the sort of more conspiratorial

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.700
elements of it, which seem to be the nighttime
shows.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:53.149
Sean HANNITY, Laura Ingram, a lot more Hunter
Biden talk and sort of this sort of thing.

00:12:53.149 --> 00:12:59.480
Daytime, I see a lot more sort of what I would
call contrived controversies that when you

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.620
look at the story, you and I probably go,
why is that even a story?

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:09.600
But it's like minor things about Joe Biden
or Kamala Harris did or didn't say or go to

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.600
a particular place.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:11.600
And they should have.

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:16.360
It seems sort of more that style of criticism
of Biden during the daytime news Is my perception

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:18.459
of that split accurate?

00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:22.790
What do we expect the coverage of Biden to
look like over the next couple of years?

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:28.740
I think what we've run into at this point
is that the interest on the right for any

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:33.740
sort of policy discussion has really just
fallen off of a cliff.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:34.740
Yes.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:44.870
Looking back at what Fox was like in 2009
or 2010, you had the sort of Tea Party push

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:49.889
for lower spending, lower taxes and so on
and so forth.

00:13:49.889 --> 00:13:54.440
And that kind of discussion of domestic policy
just doesn't happen anymore.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:14:03.000
Instead, it's been replaced with various controversies
about woke public schools and these kind of

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:11.940
very minor, very specific events that they
can dig up from local news and fit into their

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:15.399
broader narratives.

00:14:15.399 --> 00:14:19.220
And so that's that's really what I expect
to see more of in the next couple of years.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:23.350
I think they're very bored with Joe Biden
and the Biden administration that they have

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:32.250
not really bothered to create much of a narrative
against him and that that is going to have

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:37.610
a very particular impact on the Republican
presidential primary, because if they don't

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:42.779
need to talk about major economic policy issues,
they're just not going to.

00:14:42.779 --> 00:14:45.920
Yeah, there's been sort of an air of like,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:52.180
Joe Biden's old and he's woke and he's confused
and Hunter Biden is probably a problem.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.959
But did you see what happened at this drag
show in Tulsa, Oklahoma or something like

00:14:55.959 --> 00:14:56.959
that?

00:14:56.959 --> 00:14:59.360
And that actually does seem to be far more
interesting to their viewers at.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:00.510
This point.

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:01.510
Yeah.

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:05.639
And it's the sort of thing where they've trained
their viewers to want a very particular thing

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:07.610
from the news.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.570
And now that's all the viewers want.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:16.899
It's frankly, it's pretty bad for the Republican
Party that this is the case because it's leading

00:15:16.899 --> 00:15:25.149
them to be just completely unable to discuss
issues that the broad scope of the American

00:15:25.149 --> 00:15:27.660
populace wants to know about.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:35.230
Yeah, it's an opportunity for Biden If he
can get news of what he is doing to these

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:36.230
folks.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:40.050
But, you know, yesterday on my show, we talked
about the money being released for the $30

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:45.779
a month broadband credits, as well as adding
some drugs to the Medicare Part B inflation

00:15:45.779 --> 00:15:50.620
indexing list like this is really tangible,
good stuff that hits people's pocketbooks,

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:53.690
but you're not going to hear about it if you're
a primary Fox News viewer.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:55.420
And so I guess that becomes the question mark.

00:15:55.420 --> 00:16:01.480
I think it's less of an issue about reaching
those Fox viewers than it is that the message

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:10.029
that Republicans are going to make to the
overall body of voters is going to be incomprehensible

00:16:10.029 --> 00:16:15.040
to them because it is so finely tuned for
Fox viewers.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:22.070
They just don't have anything to say about
the the broad scope of domestic policy.

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:23.070
That's super interesting.

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.560
We're going to be watching that very closely.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:28.800
We've been speaking with Matt Gertz, who's
a senior fellow at Media Matters.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:31.850
Matt, really appreciate your time and your
insights today.

00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:32.370
Great to be here.

